Hold on — is zero-length actual_length actually possible for an interrupt URB? Yes: a zero-length packet (ZLP) on an interrupt IN endpoint gives actual_length == 0. A malicious/compromised USB device can send a ZLP. Also, the loop `for (j = 3; j < len; j += 2)` with len = (size_t)-1 → j iterates as int... j is declared `int j`. `j < len` compares int with size_t → j is converted to size_t (unsigned), so it stays true for all j >= 3 including negative — it would loop essentially forever reading OOB. Definite DoS/OOB read.

Wait, but which packet_id? data_in[0] is stale content from a previous packet (since actual_length==0, nothing new was written). The attacker (device) can first send a valid 0x02 status packet, then send a ZLP — data_in[0] still holds 0x02, so case 0x02 executes with len = SIZE_MAX. That's a coherent attack sequence.

